Unbound Upstream via Gateway Plugin

Forwards Unbound's root zone to a resolver that is reached through a
gateway group, using the group's best member, and switches to another
member when the best one changes. Each member can have its own resolver,
or use a default one, IPv4 or IPv6 (one family per group). The resolver
is reached by a host route and a firewall rule the plugin adds for it, so
its queries leave through that member's tunnel and never the WAN.

- When no member is usable or the resolver stops answering, the root zone
  returns to Unbound's own forwarders (or to recursion), without flushing
  the cache; a recovered member is used again only after a failback delay
- Plan and log only (dry run) is the default; nothing changes in Unbound
  or the routing table until it is turned off
- Never writes the configuration at run time: the only writer is Save
- Can POST each switch to a URL, and has a read-only status privilege
  for pollers such as Home Assistant

Plugin Changelog
================

1.5

* "Fall back to Unbound's own forwarders" (on by default) and, below it,
  "Fail closed on" (interfaces) and "Fail closed for networks" (source
  networks routed to the firewall, such as a tailnet's 100.64.0.0/10 and
  fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48). While no member is usable, a client in the set
  gets REFUSED for internet names, the others keep the fallback, and the
  firewall's own lookups from 127.0.0.1 are never refused. Three scopes:
  none (the default: checkbox on, nothing selected, nothing listed; 1.4's
  behaviour, no file, no view command, the status gains only a
  fail_closed object), selected (the interfaces' nets plus the listed
  networks) and all (checkbox off: every client except loopback). The
  two set fields are hidden while the checkbox is off. Save refuses a
  group member's interface, the loopback interface, an interface with no
  address, host bits, 0.0.0.0/0 and ::/0, and any network that overlaps
  loopback or a member's net
* How: the plugin writes unboundupstream.conf in core's
  unbound.opnsense.d, which puts the set in an Unbound view whose root
  is refused. Unbound loads it at its start, so the set is refused from
  the first query after a boot, a restart or an upgrade, and lifted once
  a member is in use (about 2.5 to 3 minutes after a boot). Falling back
  arms the refuse again before the root returns to the forwarders. The
  view follows core's Access Lists: a list inside a set net gets the
  view too, and a netblock core lists is never repeated
* Local names still resolve for a fail-closed client: host overrides and
  their PTR records, the system domain, DHCP lease names, domain
  overrides and the private reverse zones. Local zones are copied into
  the view while it is armed, so a name with no data in a local zone is
  answered NXDOMAIN or REFUSED by Unbound itself, never asked upstream.
  A name that appears after the last minute tick is refused (or
  NXDOMAIN) until the next tick. TXT records holding both quote kinds
  are not copied
* Unbound restarts once, through configd, at Apply when the set's nets
  change, when a selected interface's addresses change (a new prefix),
  and when core's Access Lists change (a second restart after core's
  own). The cache is lost on each if cache flushing is on. A reload with
  unbound-control re-arms the view; the next tick lifts or reconciles it
* A clean removal, an emptied set, or a disable with a set selected
  restarts Unbound once through configd while the view is loaded, which
  drops it. The plugin never deletes anything in /var/unbound/etc. An
  upgrade keeps the refuse and the file through its window. A stop that
  cannot restore keeps the refuse armed
* Unbound without view commands, or a view: or access-control-view line
  of the owner's own, refuses a non-empty set. A file Unbound cannot
  parse is never written
* The page shows "Fail closed" (armed, lifted, not armed with the reason,
  or stuck). The status and the notify POST carry fail_closed, the log
  lines "fail closed: ... refused (no usable member)" and "fail closed:
  lifted (...)" are at error level, and the Home Assistant package
  gains binary_sensor.dns_fail_closed
* Limits. A client querying from an IPv6 link-local address is not
  covered. A DS query at the apex of a local zone is refused. A device
  that falls back to DoH or Private Relay resolves elsewhere; only pf
  stops that. A lookup the firewall sends from an interface address in
  the set (not from 127.0.0.1) is refused. Tailnet clients: internet
  names are refused for every device that uses the firewall for DNS,
  wherever it is; local names and MagicDNS names still resolve; the
  firewall must be the tailnet's only global nameserver ("Override DNS
  servers" on, "Use with exit node" on), or another nameserver's answer
  wins; a WireGuard-only exit node such as Mullvad is refused as well;
  split DNS alone never reaches the check. Downgrading to 1.4 leaves
  the file, which 1.4 cannot lift
* Recovery, for a failed upgrade, a downgrade or a removal that could
  not finish:

      rm /usr/local/etc/unbound.opnsense.d/unboundupstream.conf
      configctl unbound restart

1.4

* A resolver per member (a row below the settings) or the default one,
  IPv4 or IPv6. A group holds one family: Save refuses a resolver of the
  other family, an IPv6 resolver while IPv6 is not allowed, or a group
  with both, and tunnel mode is refused if one gets through. With one
  default and no rows the routes, forwarders and the status fields 1.3
  had are unchanged; the other entries below still apply
* Saving a new resolver for the active member switches in place, with no
  stop first. The previous address is rejected, held for at least 60 s
  and drained, then deleted with its block; one the owner's own root
  zone names is deleted as soon as Unbound's live root is that zone
* The member passes, and the pass for the address the plugin last used,
  keep state per interface (if-bound) and accept any flags: a TCP state
  can no longer carry the resolver's packets out the WAN after the
  member route is gone, and a reused Unbound TCP connection after a
  member change is reset and reconnects instead of being blocked
* The contract also refuses tunnel mode when unbound.conf cannot be read
  (its include files are read too), when Unbound's configuration says
  do-ip4: no for an IPv4 group or do-ip6: no for an IPv6 group, when
  Outgoing Network Interfaces is set, when the group mixes IPv4 and IPv6
  members, when a resolver is of the other family than the group's, and
  when Unbound's own root zone forwards to a member's resolver
* Per-member resolver rows apply as soon as they are saved or deleted,
  through the same apply as the Apply button; the settings form still
  needs Apply
* A failed reject route, delete or drain of an old address is logged
  with its address and retried by the next run
* The status keeps 1.3's fields for one address and adds routes,
  addresses and each member's resolver
* The state file converts on read; the pass labels and the passes' state
  options change, so the rules are listed once after the upgrade
* WireGuard is the only tunnel type tested

