1.3

Security release.

* The block follows the address the plugin last used, not only the
  setting: disabling the plugin or changing the resolver address keeps
  that address blocked (and the route's device passed) until a stop
  has really undone it. The minute job stays installed while such an
  address remains, and the filter is reloaded when that address changes
* A stop that cannot restore Unbound's forwarders through its control
  channel ends in Unbound's own start, which applies the root zone you
  configured (every requested stop and the package removal; a requested
  stop does so unless the cache is flushed on a restart). Every restart
  goes through configd, or core's own script when configd does not
  answer. A removal leaves no rule behind: when even the restart leaves
  Unbound on the resolver, it makes the plugin's route a reject route
  and logs what to run. The removal waits up to 120 s for the lock and
  asks core to reload the filter and restart cron, with core's own
  scripts when configd does not answer. An upgrade still removes the old
  package first, so it falls back to Unbound's own forwarders for 30 to
  60 s
* The page decodes status text with one plain function and no HTML
  parser
* Answers from Unbound's forward list and the configured root zone's
  forward-addr values are admitted by shape only (address, optional
  port, optional host name); an unknown zone option is reported as
  "unknown option", never by its text
* Settings read from a restored configuration (resolver, probe name,
  failback delay, gateway group, notify URL) are checked again where
  they are used; curl speaks only http and https, with no redirects
* A failed notification names only the receiver's scheme, host and port
  (it used to log the whole URL)
* State, record and zone files are read as regular files only, never
  through a link, at most 1 MiB each; a zone file that cannot be read
  that way makes the root zone unrestorable, never recursion. pfctl's
  error file lives in the plugin's root-only run directory
* Control characters are replaced in log lines (a line break by "; ",
  any other by "?")
* Every copyright header reads "Copyright (C) 2026 cayossarian"

1.2_1

* A rules listing cut short by a filter reload, even one that leaves
